      "claim": "The doctor owed the father a duty of care because a doctor‑patient relationship existed.",
      "evidence": "The father was treated by the doctor in the hospital after the mother called him; this creates a legal doctor‑patient relationship.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "A breach of the duty requires a failure to meet the applicable standard of care; violating the hospital’s written rule by giving an oral order is not per se a breach of the legal standard of care.",
      "evidence": "Hospital policies are evidence of the standard of care but are not binding legal rules; a reasonable physician could still satisfy the standard despite the oral order, and the order was correctly recorded as A‑positive.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The doctor’s conduct was not the proximate cause of the father’s death because an independent negligent act by a blood‑bank employee broke the causal chain.",
      "evidence": "The employee who mislabeled the blood was a separate actor; the mislabeling directly caused the fatal transfusion, and the doctor’s oral instruction did not contribute to that error.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The blood‑bank employee who supplied the wrong blood was not an agent or employee of the doctor, so the doctor is not vicariously liable for that employee’s negligence.",
      "evidence": "The nurse and blood‑bank staff are employed by the hospital; the doctor had no control over the employee’s labeling task.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the doctor neither breached the applicable standard of care in a way that caused the injury nor is vicariously liable for the independent negligence of the blood‑bank employee, the mother’s wrongful‑death claim against the doctor will fail.",
      "evidence": "Negligence requires duty, breach, causation, and damages; breach and proximate cause are lacking.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    }
